# AL Labels and TextConsts — Suffix and `Locked`

CodeCop **AA0074** requires every `Label` / `TextConst` identifier to end with an approved suffix. The suffix signals at the call site how the text is consumed and what translation behavior it should get.

## Approved suffixes (the only valid set)

| Suffix | Used with                                                         | Example identifier                   |
| ------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `Err`  | `Error(...)`, `ErrorInfo`                                         | `CustomerNotFoundErr`                |
| `Msg`  | `Message(...)`, `Notification.Message(...)`                       | `UpdateCompleteMsg`                  |
| `Qst`  | `Confirm(...)`, `StrMenu(...)`                                    | `DeleteRecordQst`                    |
| `Lbl`  | Captions, tooltips, field captions                                | `CustomerNameLbl`                    |
| `Tok`  | Locked tokens (URLs, GUIDs, JSON keys, single-string identifiers) | `GetMethodTok`, `EmailViewPolicyTok` |
| `Txt`  | General text, telemetry message text                              | `TelemetryDataTxt`                   |

Pick the suffix that matches the actual usage. `Tok` vs `Lbl` vs `Txt` is a judgment call when more than one is valid for the case — what matters is that the suffix is on the approved list AND matches the call.

## Placeholder labels MUST declare `Comment`

When a label contains `%1`, `%2`, etc., the `Comment` property MUST document each placeholder. Translators rely on this; without it, the translation pipeline produces nonsense.

```al
MissingSecretErr: Label 'The secret %1 is either missing or empty.',
                  Comment = '%1 = Secret Name.';

EmailSentMsg: Label 'Email sent to %1 at %2.',
              Comment = '%1 = recipient email, %2 = timestamp.';
```

## `Locked = true` for non-translatable strings

Use `Locked = true` whenever the label MUST NOT be translated. This applies to:

- URLs and URIs (`https://...`)
- HTTP method names (`GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`)
- GUIDs / token strings
- JSON keys, XML element names, OData query strings
- Telemetry message text (telemetry strings are read by the BC team and external tools that don't expect translations)
- Technical identifiers used to key into the platform (e.g. `Session.LogMessage` category labels)

```al
ApiBaseUrlTok: Label 'https://api.example.com/v1', Locked = true;
GetMethodTok: Label 'GET', Locked = true;

TelemetryRetrieveEmailsUsedTxt: Label 'Retrieving emails is used', Locked = true;

EmailViewPolicyTok: Label 'Email View Policy', Locked = true;  // category for telemetry
```

When the label is shown to a user (captions, error messages, prompts), do NOT set `Locked = true` — the user-facing translation pipeline depends on these being translatable.

## Pass parameters DIRECTLY to `Error`, `Message`, `Confirm`

Never wrap the label with `StrSubstNo` at the call site, and never concatenate strings into the call.

```al
// GOOD
Error(CustomerNotFoundErr, CustomerNo);

// BAD - hides placeholders from the translation pipeline
Error(StrSubstNo(CustomerNotFoundErr, CustomerNo));

// BAD - untranslatable, unanalyzable
Error('Customer ' + CustomerNo + ' not found');
```

The platform performs the substitution itself. `StrSubstNo`-then-`Error` removes the format-string identity from the call site so analyzers cannot match the call to its label and translators lose the link.

The same rule applies to `Message`, `Confirm`, and other UI primitives.

## Detection heuristics for review

When reviewing AL code, flag:

- Any `Label` / `TextConst` whose name does NOT end with one of the six approved suffixes.
- Any `Label` with `%1`/`%2`/... but no `Comment` property.
- Any `Label` containing `://` (URL marker) but not `Locked = true`.
- Any `Label` whose name ends in `Tok` but lacks `Locked = true` (tokens by definition are not translatable).
- Any `Label` referenced inside `Session.LogMessage(...)` without `Locked = true`.
- Any `Error(StrSubstNo(...))`, `Message(StrSubstNo(...))`, or `Confirm(StrSubstNo(...))` call.
- Any `Error('literal' + var)` or `Message('literal' + var)` string concatenation.

